## Files changed

- `src/lib/api.ts` — `RunStatus` gains `"awaiting_skill_decision"`; `Run`
  gains `active_skills: string[]`; new `SkillActivationDecision` type and
  `PendingSkillActivationUi` interface; `RunEvent` gains
  `skill_activation_requested` / `skill_activation_decided` variants (field
  names match the Rust structs' literal serde output verbatim, no
  camelCasing).
- `src/lib/events.ts` — `AxisState.pendingSkillActivation`; two new
  `applyEvent` cases (set on requested, cleared on decided — keyed by skill
  name so a stale/duplicate decided event is a no-op).
- `src/store/app.ts` — `skillActivationSupported` capability flag (default
  `true`, flips `false` on a 404/405 from the new endpoint, mirroring
  `cancelSupported` exactly); `decideSkillActivation(runId, skill, decision)`.
  Unlike `cancelRun`, no manual axis patch on success — the kernel emits its
  own `SkillActivationDecided` from `decide_skill_activation`, so the WS
  event clears the pending state and flips `run.status` back to `running`
  on its own.
- `src/features/runs/SkillActivationPrompt.tsx` (new) — the inline prompt.
- `src/features/runs/Timeline.tsx` — renders the prompt when
  `axis.pendingSkillActivation` is set; header badge branches on
  `run.status === "awaiting_skill_decision"`; `aria-live` announce on
  pause (assertive) and resolution (polite); scroll-follow effect also
  triggers on the prompt's arrival.
- `src/features/runs/RunDetail.tsx` — **load-bearing fix, not optional
  polish**: the `live` check (which decides Timeline vs. Result) did not
  include the new status. Without this, a paused run would render `Result`
  instead of `Timeline`, and `Result.tsx`'s status switch defaults anything
  it doesn't recognize to `"completed"` — a paused, undecided run would have
  shown a green "Completed" badge. Fixed by adding
  `run.status === "awaiting_skill_decision"` to the `live` predicate.
- `src/features/runs/MasterList.tsx`, `src/lib/format.ts` — added the new
  status to `STATUS_CLS` / `STATUS_GLYPH` so the run-list row shows a glyph
  (`◉`, warning-tone) instead of silently rendering nothing for an unmapped
  status key.
- `apps/web-ui/design/07-open-decisions.md` — new rows D-9, D-10.
- `apps/web-ui/design/06-implementation-handoff.md` §1a — new endpoint rows
  (the decide-activation endpoint + the six Part 6 CRUD routes).

**Part 6 addendum (skills CRUD), added once the team lead closed the
contract gap:**
- `src/lib/api.ts` — `SkillView`, `SkillInstallReport` (note:
  `skipped: [string, string][]` — a Rust `Vec<(String, String)>` tuple
  serializes as a 2-element JSON array, not an object), `SkillsPolicyView`,
  `SkillsPolicyUpdate`.
- `src/store/app.ts` — `skillsManagementSupported` capability flag
  (independent of `skillActivationSupported`, per the team lead's explicit
  suggestion — a deployment can have one endpoint family without the other
  during rollout); a small shared `skillsApiCall` helper (the same
  404/405-off body was about to be repeated six times verbatim, so it was
  factored once — not a new abstraction layer, just avoiding six copies of
  one `if`) backing `fetchSkills`, `fetchSkillsPolicy`, `installSkill`,
  `removeSkill`, `activateSkill`, `deactivateSkill`, `setSkillsPolicy`.
- `src/features/settings/SkillsSettings.tsx` (new) — the management panel.
  Data (skill list, policy) is kept in **local component state**, fetched
  on mount, not in the global store — unlike `ModelConfig`'s config view,
  nothing else in the app needs to read the skills list, so there's no
  cross-cutting reason to lift it. Only the capability flag is global,
  matching the reasoning `cancelSupported`/`replaySupported` already
  established (other components need to react to those; nothing but this
  one component needs to react to `skillsManagementSupported` either, but
  it was still put in the store per the team lead's explicit ask for
  consistency with the existing flag family).
- `src/app.tsx` — renders `<SkillsSettings />` stacked below `<ModelConfig
  />` with a plain `<hr>` divider inside the existing settings branch — no
  new routing, per the standing `§5.0` decision (no Section/Card primitive
  exists in this app).

## Rendered states — plain-text description (see "Honest limitations")

**Timeline header, live run, no pending activation** (unchanged from
before this change): task title, a pulsing accent badge reading
"◐ Kelter working…", policy/elapsed/cost line below, Stop button top-right
when `cancelSupported`.

**Timeline header, `run.status === "awaiting_skill_decision"`:** the same
badge slot instead reads a static (non-pulsing) warning-tone badge
"◉ Awaiting your decision". Stop remains available (cancel is valid on a
paused run per the backend contract) — no code change was needed there,
`cancelRun`'s only gate is the capability flag, not run status.

**Inline prompt, appearing at the end of the step list** (`role="status"`,
`animate-slide-in`, same 150ms motion token as `StepCard`): a bordered card,
visually identical in weight/radius/padding to a `StepCard`, containing:
- Line 1 (body text, `font-mono` on the skill name): `"{skill} wants to
  advise on this run — {description}"`.
- Line 2 (caption2, muted): a trust `Badge` — `"✓ trusted"` (success tone,
  green) or `"○ untrusted"` (quiet tone, gray) — followed by
  `"{provenance_source_kind}: {provenance_source}"` as plain muted text.
- Line 3: three buttons, left to right — `Button variant="ghost"` labeled
  "Not this run", `Button variant="primary"` labeled "Allow this run",
  `Button variant="secondary"` labeled "Always allow". Deliberately NOT
  colored `danger`/`warning` on any button — none of the three is a
  destructive or alarming action.

**Prompt, mid-decision (`resolving` set):** the clicked button shows its
loading spinner (`Button`'s existing `loading` prop) and text; all three
buttons are `disabled` (prevents a double-submit / conflicting second
decision on the same request) until the paired WS event lands or the call
errors.

**Prompt, after the paired `skill_activation_decided` event:**
`axis.pendingSkillActivation` clears via `applyEvent`, the whole card
unmounts — no explicit "resolved" state is rendered because the run's own
next timeline event (a fresh `proposed`/`rejected`/`executed`, or the header
badge reverting to "Kelter working…") IS the resolution, matching the
brief's instruction to treat the decision as an ordinary journaled event
rather than a dialog with its own close animation.

**Prompt, decide-call failure while `skillActivationSupported` is still
true** (e.g. a transient network error): buttons re-enable, a
`role="alert"` caption-danger line appears below them:
`"Couldn't send that — {message}"`.

**Prompt, on a daemon without the endpoint** (`skillActivationSupported ===
false`, set after one real 404/405): the three buttons are replaced
entirely by a muted caption line, `"This daemon doesn't support
skill-activation decisions."` — never a dead/disabled button, per the
config-501 convention this repo already uses for Stop/replay.

**Run-list row (`MasterList`):** status glyph column shows `◉` in
`text-warning` for a paused run, same row shape as every other status (glyph
+ `sr-only` status text + task + relative time), so B-7 (glyph+text+color)
holds here too, not just in the prompt itself.

**Settings — Skills section, loading:** three shimmering `Skeleton` bars
(`h-8`), same shape as `ModelConfig`'s own loading state, directly below the
model-config block and its `<hr>` divider.

**Settings — Skills section, unsupported daemon
(`skillsManagementSupported === false`):** the component renders nothing at
all — not a disabled panel, not a banner, just absent. The divider above it
in `app.tsx` is also conditioned on the same flag, so Settings falls back to
looking exactly like it did before this feature existed (just `ModelConfig`,
no stray trailing `<hr>`).

**Settings — Skills section, transient load error (non-404/405):** heading
"Skills" plus a `Banner tone="warning"`: `"Couldn't load skills — {message}"`.

**Settings — Skills section, loaded, policy form:** a `Select` labeled "When
the model wants an untrusted skill" with three options ("Only use
already-trusted skills" / "Allow any installed skill" / "Ask before an
untrusted skill"). Choosing "Ask before an untrusted skill" reveals a second
`Select` ("If no one is watching": "Skip the skill automatically" / "Pause
the run and wait"), and choosing "Pause the run and wait" reveals a number
`Input` ("Give up waiting after (seconds)"). A "Save policy" button below,
same loading/disabled pattern as every other button in this component.

**Settings — Skills section, install form:** a URL `Input` + "Install"
button on one row, with a muted caption below: `"A local folder or bundle
installs from the command line (`kelter skill add`) — this form only takes
a URL."` — the scope-narrowing note the team lead asked for.

**Settings — Skills section, shared result line:** one `role="status"
aria-live="polite"` caption line below the install form, reused by every
action in the panel (policy save, install, activate, deactivate, remove) —
mirrors `ModelConfig`'s single result line; since every button in the
section disables while any one action is in flight, only one action's
outcome is ever showing at a time.

**Settings — Skills section, installed list, empty:** "No skills installed
yet."

**Settings — Skills section, installed list, populated:** one bordered card
per skill (same visual weight as a `StepCard`/the ask prompt): skill name
(`font-mono`), description (caption, muted), a trust `Badge`
(`✓ trusted`/success or `○ untrusted`/quiet) and an active `Badge`
(`◉ active`/accent or `○ inactive`/quiet) stacked top-right, then a muted
provenance line (`{source_kind}: {source}`), then a button row: Activate or
Deactivate (whichever applies) plus Remove. Clicking Remove swaps it inline
for `"Remove this skill? [Confirm (danger)] [Cancel (ghost)]"` — the exact
Stop-confirm idiom from `Timeline.tsx`, reused rather than reinvented, since
deleting an installed skill is the one destructive action in this panel and
deserves the same two-step guard Stop already uses.

