## Manual accessibility pass (no automated a11y gate exists in this repo —
pre-existing residual per `design/08` Part D, not newly introduced by this
change; confirmed absent: no axe/Lighthouse/playwright/test runner anywhere
in `package.json` or the repo)

- **Keyboard operability:** all three buttons are native `<button>`
  elements via the shared `Button` component — reachable by Tab in DOM
  order (ghost → primary → secondary), activatable by Enter/Space, and
  covered by the same global `focus-visible` ring utility (`focusRing`,
  2px accent + 2px offset) as every other control in the app. No custom
  keydown handling was added, so no new keyboard trap or dead zone was
  introduced. Verified by reading `Button`'s implementation
  (`components/ui/button.tsx`) — it renders a real `<button>`, not a `div`
  with a click handler — and confirming `SkillActivationPrompt` passes no
  `tabIndex`/`role` overrides that would remove it from the tab order.
- **`aria-live` wiring:** the pause announces via `announce(..., "assertive")`
  (the existing `lib/announcer.ts` singleton, same aria-live region every
  other milestone in `Timeline.tsx` already uses) so a screen-reader user
  does not have to be reading the log at the exact moment the run pauses.
  The resolution announces `"polite"` (default), consistent with the
  brief's instruction that only the pause itself must not be missed.
  `announce()` clears its live region's `textContent` before setting it
  (50ms later) specifically so a repeat announcement — e.g. the SAME skill
  being asked about twice in one run — still fires; this pre-existing
  behavior was not touched.
- **Color + glyph + text (B-7):** every place this feature adds a status
  signal pairs a glyph with a text label and a color, never color alone —
  the trust badge (`✓ trusted` / `○ untrusted`), the header badge
  (`◉ Awaiting your decision`), and the run-list glyph (`◉` + `sr-only`
  status text, matching the existing pattern for every other status).
- **Focus behavior when the prompt appears:** deliberately does NOT steal
  focus — it mounts inside the existing `role="log"` region the same way a
  new `StepCard` does, and relies on the assertive live-region announcement
  (above) rather than a focus jump, matching the brief's explicit
  instruction that this must not read as a modal/security dialog (no focus
  trap, no forced focus). This is a considered choice, not an oversight: a
  focus jump on an unpredictable async WS event would be disorienting if
  the user is mid-typing in the composer of a *different* selected run.
- **Reduced motion:** `animate-slide-in` is the same globally-gated
  keyframe every other timeline element uses
  (`@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` in `design/05`'s `@theme`
  block) — no new motion path was introduced.

**`SkillsSettings.tsx` specifically:**
- Every field uses the shared `Input`/`Select`/`Label` components, which
  already wire real `<label for>`/`id` pairs (`ModelConfig.tsx` established
  this pattern; `SkillsSettings` follows it verbatim for the policy
  selects, the timeout input, and the install URL field) — reachable and
  operable by keyboard alone, no custom widgets.
- The per-skill Remove confirm reuses the Stop-confirm shape exactly
  (danger "Confirm" + ghost "Cancel" swapped in for the single "Remove"
  button) — same keyboard/focus properties already verified for that
  pattern in `Timeline.tsx`, not a new interaction to separately audit.
- The shared result line is `role="status" aria-live="polite"`, same as
  `ModelConfig`'s — a screen-reader user gets the outcome of whichever
  action just ran without needing to locate it visually.
- Trust and active badges both pair glyph + text + color (B-7): `✓
  trusted`/`○ untrusted`, `◉ active`/`○ inactive`.
- Buttons disable as a group while any one action is in flight
  (`busy && busyKey !== "…"`), preventing a second concurrent mutation from
  a stray keyboard activation while a request is pending — not an a11y
  requirement per se, but prevents a race that would otherwise produce a
  confusing announced result.

## Honest limitations

- **No real screenshots, for either piece.** For the inline prompt: the
  backend code path that actually pauses a run and emits
  `skill_activation_requested` was still mid-implementation when this
  evidence was first written — no live paused run existed to point a
  browser at. For the Settings panel: even once the backend's Part 0-4 work
  finished, the Part 6 CRUD endpoints (`/api/v1/skills*`) were assigned to
  the backend agent to build AFTER that, and this evidence was written
  without waiting to confirm they'd landed — so there was no live
  `/api/v1/skills` response to screenshot against either. Rigging synthetic
  browser state to screenshot against (e.g. exposing the store's internal
  `state` on `window` for console injection) would have meant adding
  test-only instrumentation to production code as a side effect of this
  task, which is exactly the kind of scope creep the brief told me not to
  introduce silently. The "Rendered states" sections above are instead
  precise, code-derived textual descriptions of every state, written from
  the actual JSX/class names rather than from memory or invention.
- **No end-to-end integration test** of this frontend against either real
  backend surface — by design (concurrent-implementation constraint), not
  an oversight. Both wire contracts were followed to the letter from the
  design doc's field lists (Parts 1/2/3 for the ask flow, Part 6 for the
  CRUD routes); a first real integration pass should happen once all pieces
  are merged.
- **axe/Lighthouse:** genuinely absent from this repo (confirmed, see
  above) — not run, not claimed.

