## 1. The pause, and all three decisions — real transcripts

All 8 tests in `ask_activation_test.rs`, run individually:

```
$ cargo test -p skills --test ask_activation_test -- --nocapture
running 8 tests
test cancel_works_on_a_paused_run ... ok
test ask_pauses_and_surfaces_the_request ... ok
test ask_fallback_deny_never_pauses ... ok
test deny_lets_the_model_continue_without_the_skill ... ok
test allow_once_activates_for_this_run_only_no_trust_change ... ok
test allow_always_activates_and_elevates_trust_persistently ... ok
test paused_run_survives_a_restart ... ok
test invariant_holds_through_every_ask_decision ... ok

test result: ok. 8 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out
```

- **`ask_pauses_and_surfaces_the_request`** — a model self-activates an
  untrusted skill under `Ask`+`Queue`. Asserts, on the run RETURNED FROM
  `execute_run` itself (not a follow-up poll): `status ==
  AwaitingSkillDecision`, `pending_skill_activation.skill == "urge-echo"`,
  `run.steps.is_empty()` (nothing executed — the gate said "ask", not
  "allow"), and a `RunEvent::SkillActivationRequested` was actually emitted
  on the subscribed event channel.
- **`deny_lets_the_model_continue_without_the_skill`** — after
  `decide_skill_activation(.., Deny, "operator")` and a second
  `execute_run`, the run reaches `Completed` with `active_skills` never
  containing the denied skill, and the registry's trust is unchanged
  (`Some("untrusted")`).
- **`allow_once_activates_for_this_run_only_no_trust_change`** — after
  AllowOnce: `active_skills` contains the skill, a `activate_skill`
  `StepRecord` is present, `registry.trust_of(..)` is BYTE-FOR-BYTE
  unchanged from before the decision. A SECOND fresh run against the same
  kernel is then asked again (`AwaitingSkillDecision`) — proving the
  decision carries zero memory across runs.
- **`allow_always_activates_and_elevates_trust_persistently`** — after
  AllowAlways: `active_skills` contains the skill AND
  `registry.trust_of(..) == Some("trusted")`. A SECOND fresh run is then
  `Completed` with the skill active and NO new `AwaitingSkillDecision` pause
  — proving the persistence actually changed live gating behavior on a
  DIFFERENT run, not just that the sidecar file was touched.
- **`cancel_works_on_a_paused_run`** — `cancel_run_with_reason` on a run
  sitting in `AwaitingSkillDecision` returns `Canceled` with ZERO code
  changes to that method (a regression/contract check, not new behavior —
  the method already treated anything outside
  `{Completed, Failed, Canceled}` as cancelable).

## 2. The invariant, through every decision

`invariant_holds_through_every_ask_decision` parametrizes over
`[Deny, AllowOnce, AllowAlways]`: a skill urging the out-of-space action
`wipe_disk` is self-activated via the ask flow, each decision is applied in
turn (fresh registry/kernel per iteration), and in every case: `run.steps`
never contains a `wipe_disk` step, and (for AllowOnce/AllowAlways, where the
skill actually goes live) a `RunEvent::Rejected` mentioning `wipe_disk`
fires — the kernel's schema-validation rejects it exactly as it does for
any other unrecognized action, regardless of who authorized the skill that
suggested it.

## 3. Durability — the most important test in this extension

`paused_run_survives_a_restart`, against the REAL `storage::SqliteRunStore`
(not `InMemoryRunStore`) across two dropped-and-reopened `Kernel` instances
pointed at the same on-disk SQLite file — the same two-session idiom
`apps/desktop/src-tauri/tests/durable_store.rs` established for the desktop
shell. Session 1 pauses a run; both the store and kernel are dropped
("the daemon process ended"). Session 2 reopens at the same path
("restarted"), asserts `store.unfinished_runs()` CONTAINS the paused run id
(the load-bearing fix — without it this assertion fails and the run would
be silently orphaned forever), then calls `execute_run` again (simulating
the daemon's startup resume sweep) and asserts the pause is RE-EMITTED
(`SkillActivationRequested` seen again on a fresh subscription) with
`run.steps.is_empty()` — no step consumed, no model call made. Only then
does it decide `AllowOnce` and confirm the run actually resumes and
completes. Real transcript:

```
$ cargo test -p skills --test ask_activation_test paused_run_survives_a_restart -- --nocapture --test-threads=1
running 1 test
test paused_run_survives_a_restart ... ok
paused-run restart-survival OK: run 94ec0799-5484-4702-b8da-daaa22993e2a re-surfaced after reopen, decided, and completed
```

## 4. The timeout sweeper — bounded wait, tested deterministically

`daemon::skill_sweep::sweep_timed_out_skill_activations` was extracted from
`main()` into a standalone function specifically so this guarantee has a
real test rather than only being provable by inspection or a slow
wall-clock run. This required giving the `daemon` crate a `[lib]` target
(`src/lib.rs`, auto-discovered by Cargo alongside the existing `kelter`
binary) exposing `config`, `skill_sweep`, and `skills_controller` — the
binary itself is otherwise unchanged in behavior.

`timeout_sweeper_denies_and_resumes`
(`crates/daemon/tests/skill_sweep_test.rs`) injects `now`/`requested_at`
directly (no `sleep`, fully deterministic): a sweep called 10 seconds after
the request does nothing (`acted.is_empty()`, run still
`AwaitingSkillDecision`); a sweep called 301 seconds after (over the 300s
default timeout) force-denies (principal `"system:timeout"`) and returns
the run id; the caller then resumes it exactly as `main()`'s real sweep
loop does, and the run completes without the skill.

```
$ cargo test -p daemon --test skill_sweep_test -- --nocapture
cargo test: 1 passed (1 suite, 0.04s)
```

Also proven headless-safe by construction: `ask_fallback_deny_never_pauses`
(§1 above) — the DEFAULT posture (`ask_fallback = "deny"`) means a run under
`model_activation = "ask"` alone never pauses at all, so the sweeper is not
even needed unless an operator explicitly opts into
`ask_fallback = "queue"`.

## 5. Part 6 addendum — skills management over HTTP

`crates/api/tests/skills_controller_test.rs` (5 tests, an in-memory mock
`SkillsController` exercised over REAL HTTP via `reqwest` + a bound
`TcpListener`, mirroring `api_test.rs`'s existing harness style exactly):

```
$ cargo test -p api --test skills_controller_test -- --nocapture
cargo test: 5 passed (1 suite, 0.01s)
```

- `every_skills_route_404s_when_no_controller_is_wired` — all 7 routes
  (`GET`/`POST /skills`, `DELETE /skills/{name}`, `POST .../activate`,
  `POST .../deactivate`, `GET`/`POST /skills/policy`) return 404 when
  `ApiState.skills` is `None` — the exact capability-flag signal the
  frontend's `skillsManagementSupported` flag depends on.
- `skills_routes_are_bearer_gated` — 401 with no token, even when wired.
- `list_install_remove_round_trip` — empty list → install (200, report
  names the installed skill) → populated list → activate (204, reflected in
  a SUBSEQUENT list, not just the response body) → deactivate (204,
  likewise reflected) → remove (204, list empty again) → removing an
  already-gone skill fails cleanly (422, not a panic or 500).
- `policy_get_set_round_trip` — GET reflects the mock's initial state; POST
  with all three fields returns the update; a SUBSEQUENT GET echoes it back
  (proving the update actually persisted in the controller's own state, not
  just that the POST response echoed the request).
- `install_failure_reports_422` — a controller error surfaces as 422, not a
  500 or a silently-swallowed failure.

The RwLock-threading guarantee (a policy change actually changes GATING
behavior, not just what `get_policy()` reports) is proven separately and
more precisely against the real `RegistrySkillGate` (not a mock) in
`crates/skills/src/gate.rs`:

```
$ cargo test -p skills --lib set_policy -- --nocapture
cargo test: 1 passed (1 suite, 0.01s)
```

`set_policy_takes_effect_on_next_authorize_activation_call`: starts
`TrustedOnly` (untrusted skill → `Deny`), flips live to `Ask`+`Queue` via
`set_policy` (asserting `get_policy()` echoes the new snapshot), asserts the
VERY NEXT `authorize_activation` call returns `AskHuman` — not just that the
setter worked — then flips again to `All` and asserts the next call returns
`Allow`.

## 6. Review-standard mapping

| Criterion | Proven by |
|---|---|
| Pause + surfacing | `ask_pauses_and_surfaces_the_request` |
| All three decisions' effects | `deny_lets_the_model_continue_without_the_skill`, `allow_once_activates_for_this_run_only_no_trust_change`, `allow_always_activates_and_elevates_trust_persistently` |
| Invariant holds through every decision | `invariant_holds_through_every_ask_decision` |
| Headless / `ask_fallback=deny` never hangs | `ask_fallback_deny_never_pauses` |
| Durability survives a real restart | `paused_run_survives_a_restart` (real `SqliteRunStore`, two sessions) |
| Cancel works on a paused run | `cancel_works_on_a_paused_run` (zero code changes needed — regression check) |
| Timeout sweeper is bounded, not just inspected | `timeout_sweeper_denies_and_resumes` (deterministic, injected clock) |
| Part 6 CRUD + policy contract | `skills_controller_test.rs` (5 tests) + `set_policy_takes_effect_on_next_authorize_activation_call` |

## Judgment calls and deviations from the design doc

- **`ActivationAuthorization` derives `Clone` (not `Copy`)** since `Deny`/
  `AskHuman` carry owned `String`/struct data — the design doc's sketch
  didn't specify derives; this is the only sensible choice given the
  variants' payloads.
- **The Deny-reason/Allow-trust unwrap helpers in `skills_test.rs`**
  (`deny_reason`/`allow_trust`) were added because three PRE-EXISTING test
  call sites used `.unwrap_err()`/`.unwrap()` directly on the old
  `Result<String,String>` return — the design doc's Part 1 didn't call
  these out explicitly (they're outside the "Tests" section, which only
  lists NEW tests) but they were a necessary, mechanical consequence of the
  signature change, not a design decision.
- **`skill_sweep_test.rs` and `skills_controller_test.rs` live in
  `tests/`, not colocated with `ask_activation_test.rs`** — because the
  timeout sweeper needs the `daemon` crate specifically (which cannot be a
  dev-dependency of `skills`, that would be circular: `daemon` depends on
  `skills`) and the CRUD-router test needs `api` specifically (mirroring
  `api_test.rs`'s own harness in the crate that owns the router). This
  matches the design doc's own suggestion ("your call") for test 8's
  placement.
- **Part 6 addendum scope**: implemented as specified, including the
  explicit URL-only install-over-HTTP scope line, the `RwLock`-bundled
  `AskPolicyConfig`, the concrete-`Arc<RegistrySkillGate>`-alongside-the-
  trait-object daemon-wiring pattern, and the overlay-file persistence
  mirroring `model-config`'s. The one noted limitation (sweeper reads a
  startup snapshot of `ask_timeout_secs`/its own spawn condition, not the
  live `RwLock`) is flagged plainly in ADR 0021 rather than silently
  worked around — building live sweeper-task lifecycle management was
  judged out of proportion to what this addendum actually needed.
- **No STOP conditions were hit.** No decision required granting capability
  beyond context; every pause has a bounded, tested escape path
  (`ask_fallback=deny` for the headless case, the timeout sweeper for the
  queued case, plus `cancel_run_with_reason` for an operator who wants out
  immediately); no change to `ActionSchema::validate`'s trust boundary
  beyond what ADR 0020 already added was needed; and the
  `unfinished_runs`/durability fix, once identified per the design doc's
  Part 0, was verified sufficient by an actual restart test against real
  SQLite, not left as an assumption.
